  "summary": "The fan-deck as worldview: color standardized, licensed, and traded like stocks.",
  "description": "Pantone built the 20th century's color infrastructure: Lawrence Herbert's 1963 ink-matching cards became the Pantone Matching System — numbered swatches (PMS 485 red, 286 blue, 448C \"sepiatone\") that printers, brands and fashion pipelines treat as legal truth. The fan-deck's matte-chip presentation became an aesthetic itself: brands defend trademarked Pantones (Tiffany 1837, UPS brown), the Color of the Year shapes retail seasons, and designers quote the chip-card layout as design shorthand for authority. Pantone's systemization also carried costs — standardized sameness debates — but as visual culture the numbered chip is as canonical as the hex code.",
  "culturalContext": "Pantone standardized color as legal and commercial truth: trademarked brand colors, Color of the Year retail cycles and the fan-deck's authority aesthetic made the chip-card design's own icon — and the system's dominance still shapes debates about color standardization's costs.",
